bevy-horizon: take NVK's GPU backing from the top of the heap, downward

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at d4a00ea0ab3f59afb967cc5d779e4263d237bd77. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it; this text before
the first "diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

NVK on Horizon took every GPU buffer and image with memalign from the malloc heap the game uses.
Each needs one contiguous run, often 8-104 MiB (gpu-alloc's chunks and dedicated allocations).
After a game builds and frees its scene data, malloc's free space is hundreds of MiB of holes,
none big enough, and the next large GPU block fails with VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY while half
a GiB is free (Skate 3 run sk8f: a 16 MiB block with 508 MiB in holes; bevy-horizon
docs/platform-notes.md).

This takes backing from the top of the heap downward instead. devkitA64's sbrk (libsysbase
sbrk.c) never moves the break past fake_heap_end and rereads it on every call, so lowering
fake_heap_end hands the memory above it to the GPU with no fixed reservation; CPU memory grows up,
GPU memory down, and neither fragments the other. fake_heap_end only changes under newlib's
__malloc_lock, which malloc holds around its sbrk calls; blocks freed at the bottom of the region
go back to sbrk. A bitmap of 64 KiB granules tracks the region, so its bookkeeping never
allocates. When the region can't grow (it keeps 16 MiB free above the break), blocks come from
memalign as before. NOUVEAU_HORIZON_TOP_DOWN=false turns it off.

The process's own large allocations can share the region: nouveau_horizon_region_alloc, _free
and _stats (kept global by scripts/localize_nvk.sh) let bevy_horizon's `top_down_alloc` Rust
allocator put blocks of 256 KiB and up there. newlib's malloc is built without mmap (_mallocr.c
sets HAVE_MMAP to 0), so otherwise a game's large blocks sit between its small ones, and once it
frees them the GPU finds only small holes (Skate 3 run sk8g: a 64 MiB block failed with the region
at 706 MiB and 503 MiB free in malloc's holes).

